This repository contains scripts to automate a minimalist Arch Linux
deployment using strict hardware profiles (AMD Ryzen Desktop and Intel
Celeron Laptop). The architecture is based on a pure X11 environment
and the suckless toolset.

--- System Architecture and Justifications -----------------------------

Booting utilizes systemd-boot for native UEFI integration. Unified
Kernel Images (UKIs) are built to package the kernel, initramfs,
microcode, and arguments into a single executable. This simplifies
Secure Boot via sbctl and prevents tampering. The EFI partition (ESP)
is allocated 1GiB to accommodate multiple UKIs.

The root system is encrypted with LUKS2 and bound to the TPM 2.0 (PCR
7, the Secure Boot state) with a mandatory PIN for secure automatic
decryption. PCR 7 is chosen deliberately: unlike PCR 0/4 it survives
firmware and kernel updates, so auto-unlock keeps working after
upgrades. Because sealing to PCR 7 is only valid once Secure Boot is
actively enforcing, enrollment is deferred to a one-shot service that
fires on the first boot with Secure Boot enabled. Internally, it is
formatted as Btrfs with isolated subvolumes (@, @home, @cache, @log)
to ensure that snapshot rollbacks do not destroy the pacman cache or
log history. The snap-pac package automates protective snapshots
during pacman transactions.

The Btrfs mount options are per profile, because the two machines are
opposites on the axis that matters. The desktop's NVMe gets ssd
allocation heuristics and async discard. The laptop's 5400 RPM SATA
disk gets neither: forcing ssd would defeat Btrfs's own rotational
detection, and a hard disk has no TRIM to issue. It gets autodefrag
instead, which targets the small-random-write fragmentation that
copy-on-write produces on rotational media, a cheaper zstd:1 to spare
a 1.8 GHz part with no turbo, and commit=120 so the disk wakes less
often.

The laptop additionally carries a @swap subvolume holding a 6 GiB
swapfile, created with `btrfs filesystem mkswapfile` so the NOCOW
attribute and preallocation are handled correctly. This exists for
hibernation: systemd refuses to hibernate to zram, so a machine that
is opened and closed all day needs real disk swap to suspend into.
zram keeps the higher swap priority, so it still absorbs ordinary
paging and systemd ignores it when choosing a hibernation target.

System performance is optimized via zram-generator, with the VM
subsystem tuned for the compressed device (high swappiness, no swap
readahead). The desktop provisions half of system memory. The laptop
provisions 1.5x instead, but pairs it with zram-resident-limit so the
device may only ever consume about half the machine's physical RAM:
generous virtual capacity, hard ceiling on real footprint, which is
what 4 GB wants. zswap is disabled on the kernel command line for
both, because Arch enables it by default and, left on, it intercepts
pages before zram ever sees them.

Firmware is per profile, and named per device rather than taken in
bulk. linux-firmware is a metapackage that owns no files and pulls ten
vendor subpackages, around 407 MiB, most of it for hardware neither of
these machines has. The desktop installs the amdgpu subset; the laptop
installs the intel one, which carries the i915 GuC/HuC/DMC blobs, the
iwlwifi firmware for its AX201 and the Intel Bluetooth firmware. Both
also install the realtek subset, for unrelated reasons: the desktop
needs it for its analog audio output, the laptop for the onboard
RTL8111/8168, where r8169 loads rtl_nic/rtl8168h-2.fw. Neither
installs sof-firmware, because snd_intel_dspcfg selects the legacy HDA
path on the laptop and Sound Open Firmware would sit unused. Should
the internal microphone ever fail to capture, that package is the
first thing to try: DMIC sometimes needs SOF when playback does not.

CPU scaling is per profile. The desktop runs the performance governor
to minimize state-transition latency. The laptop runs powersave with
an EPP hint of balance_power: under HWP that is the dynamic governor,
and the real bias knob in intel_pstate's active mode is the energy
performance preference, not the governor name. Both are written to
/etc/default/cpupower-service.conf, which is the file cpupower.service
actually reads.

An I/O scheduler policy is installed on both machines, attribute
matched rather than device named: BFQ for rotational disks, none for
NVMe. It is a no-op on the desktop and the largest single
responsiveness win available on the laptop.

The laptop carries a small set of explicit power-management files
rather than TLP, so every setting stays visible in this repository:
wifi and audio runtime power saving, PCI runtime power management, a
relaxed dirty writeback interval to match commit=120, lid handling via
suspend-then-hibernate, earlyoom configured never to kill the window
manager or terminal, AC-gated Btrfs maintenance, and a battery charge
cap applied only if the model actually exposes the attribute.

Sleep is driven by the lid switch alone. logind's IdleAction is left
off, because logind has no way to observe activity here: for a session
of type tty it infers idleness from the atime of the session's TTY,
and X started by startx on tty1 takes its input from evdev and never
touches /dev/tty1. The atime freezes at login, so the session reads as
idle from that second onward and the machine sleeps on a fixed
interval regardless of what is being typed. An idle timeout has to be
measured inside X, by a user-session timer or a locker, not by logind.

PipeWire serves as the default audio backend, globally enabled for
seamless login, with bare-metal hardware gates unmuted directly at the
physical layer via ALSA. The analog card is resolved by capability
rather than by index, since probe order is not stable.

A display manager is explicitly omitted to prevent background daemon
overhead. X11 sessions are initialized manually from the TTY. The
network stack is systemd-networkd on the desktop and NetworkManager on
the laptop, which needs to roam between wireless networks; both are
heavily restricted by UFW.

--- Script Execution Flow ----------------------------------------------

The deployment is split into two distinct phases to maintain a minimal
core system.

install.sh (Phase 1 - Live ISO)
  Requires a mandatory hardware profile argument (amd or intel).
  Wipes the target disk, constructs the Btrfs on LUKS2 topology,
  bootstraps the base packages, and generates the UKIs. It transfers
  the Phase 2 scripts to the new filesystem before reboot.

post_install.sh (Phase 2 - Booted System)
  Enforces UFW rules, creates and signs Secure Boot keys, configures
  zram, and establishes Snapper policies. TPM 2.0 auto-unlock
  enrollment is deferred to a one-shot service that fires on the first
  boot with Secure Boot active (see the boot flow above). It utilizes
  interactive prompts to seamlessly compile custom PKGBUILDs without
  root privileges and delegate dotfiles deployment.

--- Testing ------------------------------------------------------------

Three layers, none of which need root on the host, described in full
in HACKING: a static lint (shellcheck plus a check that every package
name the scripts use still exists), a set of post-condition assertions
that can be run against an installed tree or a running machine, and a
VM harness that installs both profiles unattended and asserts on the
result.

  ./tests/lint.sh --strict
  ./tests/vm.sh --profile amd
  sudo ./tests/assertions.sh / amd

The VM harness runs both deployment phases: it installs from the ISO
onto a blank virtual disk, then boots that disk and runs the
post-installation on it, asserting after each. Nothing in it needs
root on the host.

post_install.sh delegates its last two steps to two other
repositories: dotfiles, which stows the config files, and pkgbuilds,
which compiles the custom packages. Clone both into tmp/ and the
harness installs from those working copies instead of from their
remotes, so a test run covers the whole deployment -- stow included,
and a real makepkg build included -- against the trees on this
machine. Without them the guest clones as it would on real hardware.
See HACKING for the details.

